To find the probability $p$ that all four slips bear the same number, we first choose the number that all four slips will have, and there are 10 possible numbers. Then, we choose 4 slips out of the 4 with that number, which can be done in $\binom{4}{4}=1$ way. The total number of ways to choose 4 slips from 40 is $\binom{40}{4}$. Therefore, $p = \frac{10 \times 1}{\binom{40}{4}} = \frac{10}{\frac{40 \times 39 \times 38 \times 37}{4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1}} = \frac{10 \times 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1}{40 \times 39 \times 38 \times 37} = \frac{1}{9139}$. For the probability $q$ that two slips bear a number $a$ and the other two bear a number $b\ne a$, we choose the number $a$ in 10 ways, and then choose 2 slips out of the 4 with that number, which can be done in $\binom{4}{2}=6$ ways. Then, we choose the number $b$ in 9 ways (since $b\ne a$), and choose 2 slips out of the 4 with that number, which can be done in $\binom{4}{2}=6$ ways. The total number of ways to choose 4 slips from 40 is still $\binom{40}{4}$. Therefore, $q = \frac{10 \times 6 \times 9 \times 6}{\binom{40}{4}} = \frac{10 \times 6 \times 9 \times 6}{\frac{40 \times 39 \times 38 \times 37}{4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1}} = \frac{10 \times 6 \times 9 \times 6 \times 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1}{40 \times 39 \times 38 \times 37} = \frac{6}{9139}$. The value of $q/p$ is then $\frac{\frac{6}{9139}}{\frac{1}{9139}} = 6$.; think; answer;6

